import — Read a data file into a frame                                       data · tier 1 · since 0.1.0 · 1.0 · exact

USAGE
    import(path, delim: Str, header: Bool = true, skip: Int = 0, na_values: List, types: Record,
           names: keep | snake = keep, quote: Str, decimal: Str, thousands: Str, encoding: Str = "utf-8")

DESCRIPTION
    Reads a file in the project (`import("data/wages.csv")`) or in a declared data root
    (`import("lfs:wages.parquet")`) and chooses the reader from the extension. This release reads delimited
    text: CSV, TSV and text files, following RFC 4180, with the delimiter detected from the first line when
    it is `;` or a tab, and with every option of a delimited file: another quote character or none
    (`quote:`), a decimal comma (`decimal: ","`, which makes `;` the usual delimiter), grouped digits
    (`thousands: "."`), and a file that is not UTF-8 (`encoding: "windows-1252"`, any name of the WHATWG
    list). The other formats of section 6.1 arrive with release 0.11.0, and their options (`format:`,
    `sheet:` and the rest) with them.

    The type of each column is guessed from its first 1,000 rows: Bool, Int, Float, Date (ISO 8601),
    DateTime or Str. A later value that does not fit stops the run naming the row, the column and the value
    (Z0606); nothing is ever coerced to `NA` silently. Empty cells, `NA` and `.` are missing, and
    `na_values:` adds codes; `Inf` and `-Inf` are infinity; `NaN` becomes `NA` with a note. Dates are read
    only in the ISO form, or with the format `types:` gives; a column that looks like dates in another form
    is read as text, with a note that proposes the `types:` entry. Variable names are kept as the file has
    them unless `names: snake` turns them into valid snake_case names. Every import prints one note saying
    what was read and what the format carries; further notes report what the reader changed or could not
    keep.

ARGUMENTS
    path        Str. The file, written with `/` from the project folder, or `root:file` for a data root. A
                path outside the project is refused (Z0701), and one that differs only in case from the
                file's real name is refused with the real spelling (Z0702).
    delim:      Str. The delimiter, one character. When omitted, `,` is used, or `;` or a tab when the first
                line has one of them and no comma.
    header:     Bool = true. Whether the first row names the columns. With `false` the columns are `v1`,
                `v2`, ….
    skip:       Int = 0. Lines to skip at the top of the file, before the header.
    na_values:  List. Values read as missing besides the empty cell, `NA` and `.`, as numbers or text:
                `na_values: [-99, -98]`.
    types:      Record. A record declaring a column's type when the guess is wrong: `types: {hours: Str}`,
                or a date format, `types: {sold: Date("%d/%m/%Y")}`. A column may be named as the file
                writes it or by the name `names: snake` gives it.
    names:      keep | snake = keep. Keep the names of the file, or turn each into a snake_case name and
                print the changes.
    quote:      Str. The quote character, one character; `"` by default. A quoted value may hold the
                delimiter and line breaks, and the quote itself written twice. `quote: ""` reads every
                character literally, for a file that never quotes.
    decimal:    Str. The decimal mark, one character; `.` by default. With `decimal: ","` the delimiter is
                `;` unless the first line shows a tab or `delim:` says otherwise, and `1,5` reads as a
                number while `1.5` is text.
    thousands:  Str. The character between groups of three digits, which a number may carry: `"."` in a
                German file, `","` in an English one with quoted values such as `"1,234"`, `" "` in a French
                one. Digits grouped any other way are text. It cannot be the decimal mark.
    encoding:   Str = "utf-8". The file's encoding when it is not UTF-8, by its name on the WHATWG list:
                `"windows-1252"` for files from Western European Windows programs, `"iso-8859-15"`,
                `"utf-16le"` for Unicode text exported by Excel, `"shift_jis"`. A byte the encoding cannot
                decode stops the import naming the row (Z0608).

VALUE
    A `Frame` with one column per variable of the file.

WARNINGS AND NOTES
    Note    the delimiter detected, a byte-order mark removed, blank lines skipped, names changed, values
            read as NA from na_values: or NaN, and a column that looks like dates read as text
    W0601   a text column holds values that differ only in Unicode normalisation form
    W0702   date-and-time values carried an offset and were converted to UTC
    Z0101   types: names a column the file does not have
    Z0401   delim:, skip:, na_values: or types: holds a value of the wrong kind
    Z0605   an empty, repeated or case-clashing column name, or a file without a header line
    Z0606   a value after the first 1,000 rows, or in a declared column, does not fit the column's type
    Z0607   a row with the wrong number of fields, or a quote never closed
    Z0608   a row that is not UTF-8
    Z0701   a path outside the project and its data roots
    Z0702   a path that differs only in case from the file's name
    Z0715   no file at the path

ACCURACY
    Exact.

SEE ALSO
    frame, describe, n_rows, variables

EXAMPLES
    > let wages = import("data/wages.csv")
    note: data/wages.csv read as CSV: 3 rows × 3 variables, types guessed; CSV carries no labels, value labels or categories (line 1)
    > wages
    wages · 3 rows × 3 variables

          id   wage   hours
      1    1   23.4      40
      2    2     19      35
      3    3   31.2      NA
    > import("data/wages.csv", na_values: [40]).mean(hours)
    35
    note: data/wages.csv read as CSV: 3 rows × 3 variables, types guessed; CSV carries no labels, value labels or categories (line 3)
    note: 1 value of hours read as NA from na_values 40 (line 3)
    warning: 2 rows with NA in hours were skipped (line 3)
    > import("data/wages_de.csv", decimal: ",", thousands: ".", encoding: "windows-1252")
    frame · 2 rows × 4 variables

          id   name      wage   hours
      1    1   Jörg    1234.5      40
      2    2   Aimée   987.25      35
    note: data/wages_de.csv read as CSV: 2 rows × 4 variables, types guessed; CSV carries no labels, value labels or categories (line 4)
    note: delimiter `;` detected from the first line (line 4)
